        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    None of these four is objectively "better." Someone who wants sales-level upside but cannot
    tolerate sales-level rejection volume usually finds customer success a genuinely stronger fit than
    forcing themselves through years of a role that fights their temperament. A closer look at
    marketing specifically is covered in
    <a href="/blog/career-options/is-marketing-a-good-career">is marketing a good career</a>, and
    someone already in sales who is weighing a lane change into marketing has a dedicated comparison
    in <a href="/blog/career-change/sales-to-marketing-career-switch-india">the sales to marketing
    career switch guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="growth-ceiling">Does sales actually have room to grow?</h2>

  <p>
    A fresher salary table alone hides the real ceiling question: does sales have genuine headroom to
    scale toward significantly higher income and seniority, or does it cap out at a fixed corporate
    rung once the easy wins are gone?
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
